<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Victory Stele of Naram-Sin</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Victory Stele of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naram-sin">Naram-Sin</a> is a carved stone monument celebrating the Akkadian king Naram-Sin's victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lullubi">Lullubi</a> mountain people, showing the king ascending a mountain above his defeated enemies. It is a masterpiece of Akkadian art. In the twelfth century BCE the Elamite king Shutruk-Nakhunte carried it off as booty to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>, where it was rediscovered, and it is now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/victory-stele-of-naram-sin.jpg" alt="Victory Stele of Naram-Sin" width="244" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shonagon · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St%C3%A8le_de_Naram-Sin_-_Mus%C3%A9e_du_Louvre_Antiquit%C3%A9s_orientales_SB_4_;_AS_6065.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
